  "result_text": "I'll read the campaign attempt artifact end-to-end and extract the requested fields verbatim.# ATT-0048 \u2014 End-to-End Extraction\n\n## 1. Identity and theorem\n\n**id:** `ATT-0048`  \n**subproblem_id:** `C66-GEO-Z`  \n**lane:** `geometry`  \n**campaign_id:** `C66-001`  \n**campaign_revision:** `4`  \n**packet_id:** `C66-001-v4`  \n**task_id:** `TASK-C66-M-001`  \n**target_claim_id:** `RED-0001`  \n**result_type:** `lemma`  \n**engine:** `claude`  \n**schema_version:** `3`  \n**context_revision:** `2`\n\n**theorem_statement (verbatim, full):**\n\n> Let B be a locally Noetherian scheme (no reducedness, irreducibility or characteristic hypothesis), pi : C -> B smooth proper with geometrically connected fibres of genus g >= 1, and x_1,...,x_n : B -> C pairwise disjoint sections with n >= 1, Gamma := sum_i x_i(B). Let W be locally free of rank 2 on C equipped with a short exact sequence of O_C-modules 0 -> W_4 -> W -> W_5 -> 0 with W_4, W_5 invertible of fibrewise degrees d_4, d_5 satisfying (D1) d_4 - n > 2g - 2 and (D2) d_5 - n = 2g - 2. Let phi : pi_*W -> pi_*(W|_Gamma) be evaluation, Z_ev := V(Fit_0(coker phi)) the Casnati-Ekedahl evaluation-failure locus with its determinantal/Fitting scheme structure, N := omega_{C/B} tensor W_5^vee(Gamma) (invertible of fibrewise degree 0 by (D2)), a_N : B -> Pic^0_{C/B} its classifying morphism and e the identity section. Then: (T1) pi_*W and pi_*(W|_Gamma) are locally free of ranks d_4 + d_5 + 2 - 2g and 2n, so Fit_0(coker phi) = I_{2n}(phi); (T2) on P := Pic^0_{C/B}, with L_univ := omega_{C_P/P}(Gamma_P) tensor Poin^{-1} and Phi : p_*L_univ -> p_*(L_univ|_{Gamma_P}) the universal evaluation, one has the exact equality of ideal sheaves J := Fit_0(coker Phi) = I_e, both inclusions, over every such B; (T3) Fit_0(coker phi) = Fit_0(coker phi_5) with phi_5 : pi_*W_5 -> pi_*(W_5|_Gamma), using only the filtration and never a splitting W = W_4 direct-sum W_5; (T4) Fit_0(coker phi) = a_N^{-1}(I_e).O_B, i.e. Z_ev = a_N^{-1}(e(B)) as closed subschemes of B; (T5) formation of both sides commutes with arbitrary base change, and for every locally Noetherian B-scheme T the map T -> B factors through Z_ev if and only if N|_{C_T} is relatively trivial (isomorphic to pi_T^*M for some invertible M on T), whence the identification descends verbatim to any locally Noetherian algebraic stack carrying this data, via any smooth atlas; and (T6) for (g,n) = (6,6) the balanced tetragonal Casnati-Ekedahl datum satisfies the hypotheses: with gamma : C -> P^1_B finite flat of degree 4, relations bundle F fibrewise O(4) direct-sum O(5), the canonical sub-line-bundle L_5 := O_{P^1}(5) tensor q^*q_*(F(-5)) subset F and its quotient L_4 induce a canonical filtration 0 -> W_4 -> W -> W_5 -> 0 of W := (O_{P(E^vee)}(2) tensor gamma^*F^vee)|_C with d_4 = 20 and d_5 = 16 (SRC-0004 Lemma 10.6 convention W_f = (O(2) tensor gamma^*O(-f))|_C), so that (D1) reads 14 > 10 and (D2) reads 10 = 2g-2, phi has ranks 26 -> 12, and Z_ev = a_N^{-1}(e(B)) with N = omega_{C/B} tensor W_5^vee(Gamma), equivalently Z_ev is the scheme-theoretic locus where O_C(Gamma) is isomorphic to W_5 tensor omega_{C/B}^{-1} relatively to B.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Proved steps / lemmas (numbered, exact statements)\n\n### Claims (status: proved)\n\n**1. CLM-0048-1**  \n> For pi : C -> B smooth proper with geometrically connected genus-g (g>=1) fibres over a locally Noetherian B, and Gamma the sum of n>=1 disjoint sections, the universal evaluation Phi : p_*L_univ -> p_*(L_univ|_{Gamma_P}) on P = Pic^0_{C/B} with L_univ = omega_{C_P/P}(Gamma_P) tensor Poin^{-1} satisfies Fit_0(coker Phi) subset I_e.\n\nJustification:  \n> Step 3: base change of Fitting ideals along e plus coker(e^*Phi) = R^1pi_*omega_{C/B} tensor M_0^{-1}, invertible, so Fit_0 = 0 and e^*J = 0. No support/radical argument used.\n\n**2. CLM-0048-2**  \n> In the same setting, I_e subset Fit_0(coker Phi); hence Fit_0(coker Phi) = I_e exactly, as ideal sheaves, over arbitrary possibly nonreduced locally Noetherian bases and in arbitrary characteristic.\n\nJustification:  \n> Step 4: at every point b of B and every nonzero xi in T_{e(b)}Pic^0_{C_b/k(b)} = H^1(C_{k(b)},O), coker(iota_xi^*Phi) = H^1(omega tensor N_xi^{-1}) has length 1 by perfectness of the Serre pairing over k(b), so iota_xi^*J = (eps); hence the image of J spans (I_e/I_e^2) tensor k(b); Nakayama over A = O_{B,b} gives I_e = J + I_e^2, and Nakayama over O_{P,e(b)} gives I_e = J. Points off e(B) are handled by the fibrewise cokernel computation.\n\n**3. CLM-0048-3**  \n> Given 0 -> W_4 -> W -> W_5 -> 0 with fibrewise degrees d_4, d_5 and d_4 - n > 2g-2, one has coker(phi) isomorphic to coker(phi_5) and hence Fit_0(coker phi) = Fit_0(coker phi_5); no splitting W = W_4 direct-sum W_5 is required.\n\nJustification:  \n> Step 5: restriction to Gamma is exact (locally split sequence), pi_* on Gamma is exact (Gamma affine over B), the top row is exact because R^1pi_*W_4 = 0 (d_4 > 2g-2), coker phi_4 = R^1pi_*(W_4(-Gamma)) = 0 (d_4-n > 2g-2, descended by base change plus Nakayama), then the snake lemma. Degree vanishings cited explicitly per FND-0099.\n\n**4. CLM-0048-4**  \n> With d_5 - n = 2g-2 and N := omega_{C/B} tensor W_5^vee(Gamma) of fibrewise degree 0, Z_ev := V(Fit_0(coker phi)) equals a_N^{-1}(e(B)) as closed subschemes of B.\n\nJustification:  \n> Step 6: W_5(-Gamma) = omega_{C/B} tensor N^{-1} (line-bundle algebra), base change of coker Phi along a_N, invariance of Fit_0 under twist by an invertible sheaf, and CLM-0048-2. Equality of ideals, not of point sets.\n\n**5. CLM-0048-5**  \n> Z_ev represents the subfunctor of relative triviality of N: for every locally Noetherian B-scheme T, T -> B factors through Z_ev iff N|_{C_T} is isomorphic to pi_T^*M for some invertible M on T. Consequently the identification holds verbatim over any locally Noetherian algebraic stack carrying the data, via any smooth atlas.\n\nJustification:  \n> Step 6: both implications follow by applying the scheme-level theorem over T, using that all constructions commute with arbitrary base change; the stack statement follows from smooth descent for quasi-coherent ideals together with the T-point criterion, supplying the atlas/descent argument the constraint requires.\n\n**6. CLM-0048-6**  \n> For a balanced tetragonal family (F fibrewise O(4) direct-sum O(5)), L_5 := O_{P^1}(5) tensor q^*q_*(F(-5)) is a canonical sub-bundle of F with locally free quotient L_4, inducing a canonical filtration 0 -> W_4 -> W -> W_5 -> 0 of W = (O_{P(E^vee)}(2) tensor gamma^*F^vee)|_C with W_5 the quotient built from gamma^*L_5^vee.\n\nJustification:  \n> Step 7: q_*(F(-5)) is invertible and commutes with base change since h^0 = 1, h^1 = 0 fibrewise; the counit is fibrewise the inclusion of the O(0) summand, so the twisted map L_5 -> F has fibrewise locally free cokernel. Dualise and twist, using exactness of gamma^* (gamma finite flat).\n\n**7. CLM-0048-7**  \n> At (g,n) = (6,6) the balanced tetragonal CE datum satisfies (D1) 14 > 10 and (D2) 10 = 2g-2 with d_4 = 20, d_5 = 16; phi has ranks 26 -> 12, Fit_0(coker phi) = I_12(phi), and Z_ev = a_N^{-1}(e(B)), i.e. scheme-theoretically the locus where O_C(Gamma) = W_5 tensor omega_{C/B}^{-1} relatively to B.\n\nJustification:  \n> Step 7, specialising CLM-0048-3,4,5,6 using the SRC-0004 Lemma 10.6 degree formula deg(W_f|_C) = 4g+12-4f at g = 6.\n\n### Inline theorem-level conclusions (T1)\u2013(T6) and intermediate identities\n\nFrom the argument body, the following exact mathematical statements are established:\n\n**(T1)**  \n> Fit_0(coker phi) = I_{2n}(phi).  \n> pi_*W locally free of rank chi(W) = d_4 + d_5 + 2(1-g); pi_*(W|_Gamma) locally free of rank 2n.\n\n**(2.1)**  \n> coker Phi = R^1 p_*(omega_{C_P/P} tensor Poin^{-1})\n\n**(3.1)**  \n> coker(e^*Phi) = R^1pi_*(omega_{C/B}) tensor M_0^{-1} = M_0^{-1}  \n> hence Fit_0(coker(e^*Phi)) = 0, so **J subset I_e**.\n\n**(4.1)**  \n> iota_xi^*J = Fit_0(coker(iota_xi^*Phi)) = (eps)  for every nonzero xi in T_z P_b.\n\n**(4.2)**  \n> V = (I_e/I_e^2) tensor_A k.\n\n**(4.3)**  \n> (I_e)_z = J_z + (I_e)_z^2.\n\nThen by second Nakayama: **J_z = (I_e)_z** for every z, so **J = I_e** (T2).\n\n**(5.1)**  \n> Fit_0(coker phi) = Fit_0(coker phi_5)  \n> (and coker phi = coker phi_5; coker phi_5 = R^1pi_*(W_5(-Gamma))).\n\n**(6.1)**  \n> W_5(-Gamma) = omega_{C/B} tensor N^{-1}.\n\n**(T4)**  \n> Z_ev = a_N^{-1}(e(B)) as closed subschemes of B.  \n> Fit_0(coker phi) = a_N^{-1}(I_e).O_B.\n\n**(T5)** T-point criterion:  \n> If N|_{C_T} = pi_T^*M' is relatively trivial then Fit_0(coker phi_T) = 0 and T -> B factors through Z_ev.  \n> Conversely if T -> B factors through Z_ev then N|_{C_T} is relatively trivial.  \n> So Z_ev represents the subfunctor of relative triviality of N; descends to stacks via smooth atlas.\n\n**(T6) at (6,6):**  \n> d_4 = 20, d_5 = 16; (D1) 14 > 10; (D2) 10 = 2g-2; ranks 26 -> 12; Fit_0 = I_12(phi);  \n> Z_ev = a_N^{-1}(e(B));  \n> T -> B factors through Z_ev iff O_{C_T}(Gamma_T) = W_5|_{C_T} tensor omega_{C_T/T}^{-1} tensor pi_T^*M for some invertible M on T.\n\n**Separation of status (argument \u00a78):**  \n> **Proved here (no undischarged dependency):** (T1)-(T6), from the standard inputs (S1)-(S8).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Source-backed inputs / citations\n\n### Direct source / proof dependencies\n- **SRC-0004** (in `source_ids`, `proof_dependencies`)\n- **SRC-0004 Lemma 10.6** (campaign source input, used only in Step 7):  \n  > giving the CE convention W_f = (O_{P(E^vee)}(2) tensor gamma^*O_{P^1}(-f))|_C, deg(W_f|_C) = 2(2g-2+4.2) - 4f = 4g+12-4f, and the direct-sum decomposition of the obstruction space.\n\n### Packet findings cited (orientation / constraints; not load-bearing for the proof)\n- **FND-0046** \u2014 fibrewise geometric-point equality does not suffice; Fitting-ideal comparison required\n- **FND-0047** \u2014 Proj of pi_*L linear-system identification (blocked; not used)\n- **FND-0052** \u2014 closed pure-codim-4 Z in M_{6,6} / Gy